<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ostsiedlung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th–14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as East settlement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ostsiedlung, East settlement, was the medieval migration of ethnic Germans into areas populated by Slavic, Baltic and Uralic peoples, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>. Larger treks of settlers moved east from the mid-12th century, often invited by local princes, and towns were granted German town law, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> laws; the movement ended in the beginning of the 14th century. In the 20th century German nationalists, most notoriously the Nazis, exploited it for territorial claims, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it was reversed east of the Oder as Germans were driven out.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lords who needed settlers to clear forests and marshes, or to farm the lands east of the Elbe opened by German settlement (the <b>Ostsiedlung</b>), offered them easier terms.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li></ul></section>
